SED
.............

Display a file
sed '' file.txt

Same basic purpose as:

cat file.txt
2. Replace text — most important

Suppose file.txt contains:

Hello Sunil
Hello Ravi
Hello Sunil

Replace Sunil with Kumar:

sed 's/Sunil/Kumar/' file.txt

Output:

Hello Kumar
Hello Ravi
Hello Sunil

Notice: only the first occurrence on each line is replaced.

Replace all occurrences
sed 's/Sunil/Kumar/g' file.txt

g = global replacement.

3. Modify the actual file

By default, sed only prints the modified output. It does not change the original file.

To change the file:

sed -i 's/Sunil/Kumar/g' file.txt

-i = edit the file in place.

4. Delete lines
Delete line 2
sed '2d' file.txt
Delete lines 2–5
sed '2,5d' file.txt
Delete lines containing ERROR
sed '/ERROR/d' application.log
Delete blank lines
sed '/^$/d' file.txt
5. Print specific lines
Print line 5
sed -n '5p' file.txt

-n prevents normal output.

Print lines 5–10
sed -n '5,10p' file.txt
Print lines containing ERROR
sed -n '/ERROR/p' application.log
6. Insert a line

Insert before line 2:

sed '2i\New Employee' file.txt

i = insert.

7. Append a line

Add after line 2:

sed '2a\New Employee' file.txt

a = append.

8. Replace an entire line

Suppose:

Sunil Java
Ravi AWS
Amit React

Replace line 2:

sed '2c\Ravi SpringBoot' file.txt

c = change.

Output:

Sunil Java
Ravi SpringBoot
Amit React


Real-time DevOps example
..............................
Suppose your application configuration contains:

server.port=8080
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/testdb

Change port from 8080 to 9090:

sed -i 's/server.port=8080/server.port=9090/' application.properties

This is useful in deployment/configuration automation.

10. sed with logs

Show only lines containing ERROR:

sed -n '/ERROR/p' application.log

Remove DEBUG lines from output:

sed '/DEBUG/d' application.log

Replace ERROR with PROBLEM:

sed 's/ERROR/PROBLEM/g' application.log


Command           	Purpose	         Example
sort	          Sort lines	     sort file.txt
sort -r	          Reverse sort	         sort -r file.txt
sort -n            	Numeric sort	sort -n numbers.txt
